  annotation = {中文辅助译名：EgoSEV：一种以自我为中心的社会关系和情感因果推理的社会证据验证方法},
  abstract = {EgoLink Challenge 2026 Track 1 evaluates egocentric social intelligence through multiple-choice questions that require models to infer social relationships, emotional causality, behavioral intentions, and social contexts from first-person videos and their associated audio. Unlike conventional video question answering, this task is highly sensitive to language priors, scene stereotypes, cross-modal ambiguity, and plausible-but-unsupported distractors. To address these challenges, we present EgoSEV, an Egocentric Social Evidence Verification method that reformulates answer prediction as question-guided evidence construction followed by candidate-wise verification. EgoSEV first builds a question-conditioned social evidence graph from visual, audio, temporal, affective, and relation cues. It then verifies each candidate answer based on supporting, contradictory and missing evidence and further improves reliability through source-traceable reranking, low-confidence retraction, anti-stereotype diagnosis, and all-option evidence calibration. On VAL-1717 validation set, EgoSEV achieves 92.89% accuracy, improving over the direct single-model baseline by 9.26 percentage points. On the official hidden test set, EgoSEV achieves 92.63% accuracy, securing first place on the CodaBench Track 1 leaderboard in the final testing stage. These results show that stable high-score gains come not only from stronger multimodal backbones but also from traceable, falsifiable, and evidence-calibrated so},
}

